        <h3 class="text-lg font-medium mb-4">Notes</h3>
        <ol class="text-sm text-ink-light space-y-3 list-decimal pl-5">
          <li id="fn1">The distinction between end-oriented and means-oriented objectives builds on classic work in organizational theory, particularly the means-ends inversion problem documented across multiple fields (Michels' "iron law of oligarchy," Merton's "displacement of goals").</li>
          <li id="fn2">The three-level architecture — constitutional, institutional, operational — synthesizes ideas from constitutional economics (Buchanan), institutional theory (North, Meyer and Rowan), and organizational design. The key insight is that different levels require different governance mechanisms and different revision triggers.</li>
          <li id="fn3">The measurement paradox (better data makes execution answerable but exposes objective-selection as the contested domain) connects to debates about the limits of evidence-based management and the irreducible role of judgment in strategy.</li>
          <li id="fn4">Drift and rigidity are my framing of a classic organizational pathology: organizations that revise purpose too easily while defending outdated methods too firmly. Good architecture prevents both by distinguishing what should be stable from what should adapt.</li>
          <li id="fn5">Whether organizational architecture can be deliberately designed or must emerge through political contestation remains contested. The evidence suggests that clarity about levels matters more than the mechanism that produced the clarity.</li>
        </ol>
